- heart disease, or a recent heart attack;
- a bleeding or blood clotting disorder;
- seizures or epilepsy;
- narrow-angle glaucoma;
- long QT syndrome;
- drug addiction or suicidal thoughts; or
- bipolar disorder (manic depression).
Some young people have thoughts about suicide when first taking an antidepressant. Your doctor should check your progress at regular visits. Your family or other caregivers should also be alert to changes in your mood or symptoms.
Taking trazodone during pregnancy could harm the baby, but stopping the medicine may not be safe for you. Do not start or stop this medicine without asking your doctor.
If you are pregnant, your name may be listed on a pregnancy registry to track the effects of trazodone on the baby.
It may not be safe to breast-feed while using this medicine. Ask your doctor about any risk.
Trazodone is not approved for use by anyone younger than 18 years old.

## Before taking this medicine
You should not use levothyroxine if you have an untreated or uncontrolled adrenal gland disorder.
You should not use certain liquid or injectable forms of levothyroxine if you are allergic to glycerin or edetate disodium.
To make sure this medicine is safe for you, tell your doctor if you have ever had:
- a thyroid nodule;
- thyroiditis (inflammation of the thyroid gland);
- heart problems such as a heart attack, stroke, angina, or an irregular heartbeat;
- a blood clot or a blood clotting disorder;
- diabetes (your diabetes medicine may need to be adjusted);
- anemia (low red blood cells);
- weak bones (osteoporosis), or low bone mineral density;
- problems with your pituitary or adrenal gland;
- an allergy to any food or drugs;
- recently received radiation therapy with iodine (such as I-131); or
- kidney disease.
Tell your doctor if you are pregnant or plan to become pregnant. Having hypothyroidism during pregnancy may increase the risk of miscarriage, high blood pressure in pregnancy, pre-eclampsia, stillbirth, or premature birth, and untreated hypothyroidism can affect the baby's brain development. The benefit of treating hypothyroidism outweighs these risks, and levothyroxine should not be stopped during pregnancy.
Your dose needs are often higher during pregnancy. Your doctor will check your thyroid blood tests as soon as your pregnancy is confirmed and at least once in each trimester, and will usually put you back on your pre-pregnancy dose straight after you give birth, with another blood test 4 to 8 weeks later.
Tell your doctor if you are breastfeeding. Levothyroxine passes into breast milk, but no harmful effects on breastfed babies have been reported. Treating hypothyroidism properly may also help milk supply if it is low. Your dose needs may be different while you are nursing.
You may be more likely to have a broken bone while using levothyroxine, particularly if your dose is higher than you need. Talk with your doctor about ways to keep your bones healthy.

## What other drugs will affect atorvastatin?
Sometimes it is not safe to use certain medicines at the same time. Some drugs can affect your blood levels of other drugs you use, which can increase the risk of serious muscle problems or make the medicines less effective.
Tell your doctor about all your current medicines. Many drugs can interact with atorvastatin, especially:
- other cholesterol-lowering medicines - gemfibrozil, niacin, fenofibrate, fenofibric acid, and others;
- colchicine;
- antibiotic or antifungal medicine - rifampin, erythromycin, clarithromycin, itraconazole, ketoconazole, posaconazole, and voriconazole;
- birth control pills;
- medicine to prevent organ transplant rejection; or
- antiviral medicine to treat hepatitis C, HIV, or cytomegalovirus (such as letermovir).
This list is not complete, and many other drugs may interact with atorvastatin. This includes prescription and over-the-counter medicines, vitamins, and herbal products. Not all possible drug interactions are listed here.

## Cyclobenzaprine interactions
The most serious cyclobenzaprine (Flexeril) drug interactions are with MAO inhibitors, which must never be combined with cyclobenzaprine, and with antidepressants and other serotonergic medicines, which can cause serotonin syndrome. Cyclobenzaprine also interacts with alcohol and other medicines that cause drowsiness, making sedation worse. 1,2,3
Other medicines and substances that interact with cyclobenzaprine include: 1,2,3
- MAO inhibitors such as isocarboxazid, linezolid, phenelzine, rasagiline, selegiline, and tranylcypromine (contraindicated; do not use within 14 days);
- antidepressants of any kind, including SSRIs, SNRIs, and tricyclics (risk of serotonin syndrome);
- bupropion, tramadol, or meperidine;
- verapamil;
- alcohol;
- opioid medicines, sleeping pills, other muscle relaxers, and medicines for anxiety or seizures;
- barbiturates and other CNS depressants;
- anticholinergic medicines;
- guanethidine or similar blood pressure medicines;
- cold or allergy medicines containing an antihistamine.
Cyclobenzaprine may increase the risk of seizures in people taking tramadol, and may block the blood pressure-lowering effect of guanethidine and similar drugs. 1,2,3
Tell your doctor about all your other medicines. This list is not complete, and other drugs may interact with cyclobenzaprine, including prescription and over-the-counter medicines, vitamins, and herbal products.
Can you take ibuprofen with cyclobenzaprine?:
There is no listed drug interaction between cyclobenzaprine and ibuprofen, and the two are commonly prescribed together for painful muscle injuries, since they work in different ways. In studies, taking cyclobenzaprine with aspirin did not change blood levels of either drug, and combining it with naproxen was well tolerated, although the combination caused more drowsiness than naproxen alone. Check with your doctor or pharmacist before combining medicines, especially if you take other drugs as well. 1
Can you take Tylenol with cyclobenzaprine?:
There is no listed interaction between cyclobenzaprine and acetaminophen (Tylenol), and they may be used together when recommended by your doctor. Be careful with combination cold or nighttime products, which may contain antihistamines or other sedating ingredients that do interact with cyclobenzaprine. 1

